---
import SiteLayout from '../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
import { BASE, course, SOURCE_REVISION } from '../lib/course';
---
<SiteLayout title="About, sources, and scope" wide>
  <article class="simple-page">
    <h1>Understand the chain.<br />Keep its limits in view.</h1>
    <p class="intro">From Molecule to Machine is an independent educational course connecting electronic structure, quantum algorithms, scientific validation, and machine-resource assumptions.</p>
    <section><h2>The full course, not a summary</h2><p>The original technical lesson text is preserved in the reading column and in downloadable Markdown. The concept plates, mini-labs, glossary, and knowledge checks are explicitly supplementary. They are designed to explain the lessons, not replace source review or a validated scientific calculation.</p><p>The site contains {course.lessons.length} complete lessons. Reading-time estimates use each lesson's word count at 180 words per minute; equations and code can take longer.</p></section>
    <section><h2>What the interactive models can establish</h2><p>The small models implement deliberately limited mathematics: exact combinatorial counts, a real symmetric two-state eigenproblem, ideal one-qubit interference, independent Pauli sampling uncertainty, qubit-wise compatible grouping, phase grids and aliasing, additive error bounds, a schematic physical-qubit scaling rule, and an information-handoff checklist.</p><p>They do not run QDK Chemistry, calculate a molecular energy, query a quantum device, certify chemical accuracy, or provide a real quantum resource estimate. Their assumptions are visible beside the controls. No scientific result or quantum advantage is claimed from these demonstrations.</p></section>
    <section id="sources-and-attribution"><h2>Sources and attribution</h2><p>Most source-grounded implementation explanations refer to <a href={`https://github.com/${course.sourceRepository}/tree/${SOURCE_REVISION}`}>{course.sourceRepository} at revision <code>{SOURCE_REVISION}</code></a>. Each lesson retains its source paths, linked citations, and caveats. Additional public QDK source references in resource chapters retain their own pinned revisions and version boundaries.</p><p>Source descriptions are revision-specific. Examples shown in a lesson are not evidence that the code was executed here or that a newer package version has the same behavior.</p><p>QDK Chemistry source excerpts are attributed to Microsoft Corporation and distributed under the source project's MIT license. The course's original explanations and diagrams are distinct from those excerpts. See the <a href="https://github.com/microsoft/qdk-chemistry/blob/97a3ff4fb86ebd5a2516e3548cece69127fd3555/LICENSE">upstream license</a> and <a href={`${BASE}third-party-notices.txt`}>third-party notices</a> for license texts and dependencies.</p><p>This site is not affiliated with, sponsored by, or endorsed by Microsoft. Team handoffs are functional descriptions of specialist responsibilities, not claims about an internal organizational chart.</p></section>
    <section><h2>Reading mathematics and code</h2><p>Equations are rendered locally with KaTeX and include MathML for assistive technologies. The source's math delimiters are normalized outside inline and fenced code only. Code samples remain selectable text; they are never automatically executed.</p><p>Wide tables, code, equations, and concept plates scroll within their own region. You can print a lesson without the navigation chrome. Use the theme selector for light, dark, or your system setting; motion preferences are respected.</p></section>
    <section><h2>Private progress, public lessons</h2><p>All lesson text and site source are publicly accessible. Learning progress is stored only in your browser. There are no accounts, tracking pixels, remote analytics, or AI-service calls. The hosting provider necessarily receives normal requests for the site files.</p><p>If storage is unavailable or full, the site says so and keeps the current page usable. Visit <a href={`${BASE}saved/`}>Your learning</a> to see or clear local records. A quiz score never changes the read marker automatically.</p></section>
    <section><h2>Revisit the evidence</h2><p>Use <a href={`${BASE}glossary/`}>the glossary</a> for vocabulary, the search control for full-text section matches, and each lesson's source notes for provenance. A good outcome is not simply reaching the last chapter: it is being able to say what a calculation assumed, what it produced, and what another team still needs to know.</p><p><a href="https://github.com/Rohit-2002-28/molecule-to-machine">View this independent project's public source</a></p></section>
  </article>
</SiteLayout>
